/ActualText (Information security professionals often need to consider confidentiality, integrity, and availability in their organizations. These examples help you think through the three components of the CIA triad to make your system more robust.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
124 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (Examples of confidentiality)
/E (Examples of confidentiality)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
125 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText <>
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
126 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (To prevent security breaches, confidentiality policies must be followed so access is limited only to authorized users. Data can be classified, labelled, or encrypted to allow restrictions. The IT team can implement multi-factor authentication systems. Employees can receive onboarding training to recognize potential security mistakes and how to avoid them.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
127 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (www.linkedin.com/in/farhathnathvi)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
128 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Effective information security considers who receives authorization and the appropriate level of confidentiality. For example, the finance team of an organization should be able to access bank accounts, but most other employees and executives should not have access to this information. Some security measures include locked cabinets to limit access to physical files and encrypted digital files to protect information from hackers.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
129 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Confidentiality can be compromised unintentionally. For example, IT support might accidentally send a password to multiple employees, instead of the one who needs it. Users might share their credentials with another employee, or forget to properly encrypt a sensitive email. A thief might steal an employee's hardware, such as a computer or mobile phone. Insufficient security controls or human error are also examples of breached confidentiality.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
130 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (Examples of Integrity)
/E (Examples of Integrity)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
131 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (An information system with integrity tracks and limits who can make changes to minimize the possible damage that hackers, malicious employees, or human errors can do. )
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
132 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText <>
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
133 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Information on an organization's website should be trustworthy. In another example, a company website that provides bios of senior executives must have integrity. If it is inaccurate or seems botched, visitors may be reluctant to trust the company or buy its products. If the company has a high profile, a competitor might try to damage its reputation by hacking the website and altering descriptions.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
134 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (www.linkedin.com/in/farhathnathvi)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
135 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (To protect data integrity, encryption, digital signatures, and hashing can be used. Websites can use certificate authorities that verify its authenticity so customers feel comfortable browsing and purchasing products.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
136 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (Examples of availability)
/E (Examples of availability)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
137 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (All organizations have designated employees with access to specific data and permission to make changes. Therefore, security framework must include availability.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
138 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Information security professionals must balance availability with confidentiality and integrity. For example, all employees of an organization might have access to the company email system, but detailed financial records may only be made available to top-level leadership. Those leaders should be able to access that data when they need to, and it shouldn't take too much time or effort to access it.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
139 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Backup systems should be in place to allow for availability. For example, disaster recovery systems need to be implemented so employees can regain access to data systems if there is a power outage. Or, if a natural disaster such as a hurricane or snowstorm prevents employees from physically getting to the office, their data be available to them through cloud system storage.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
140 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Availability can be compromised through sabotage. For example, sabotage can occur through denial-of-service attacks or ransomware. To maintain data availability, organizations can use "redundant" networks and servers that are programmed to become available when the default system breaks or gets tampered with. Updating and upgrading systems on a regular basis prevents infiltrations and malfunctions which enhance data availability.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
141 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (www.linkedin.com/in/farhathnathvi)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
